2026-08-25T06:02:34Z	tried	what=the sweep was spending the archive search's pre-committed budget	config=budget intervals now carry a budget_key from the job; totals are per budget; legacy rows read as 'unattributed' rather than being absorbed; job.rec sets budget_key = resim-sweep	produced=the archive search's budget reads 0 evals / 0s, which is true — it has never run under this harness. The 9705 evals and 5h 5m of productive time are the sweep's, and tmhaul budget now says so on a separate line	claim=MEASURED	control=three tests: two budgets in one directory do not contaminate each other while the cross-budget total still sums both; a keyless legacy interval lands in 'unattributed' and NOT in the search's; and ten productive hours of the wrong workload does not trip the switch for the right one	why=The harness was built so a stall could not spend this budget, and then it spent five hours of it on the wrong workload — I caught it at 50.8%, about five hours before it would have announced 'the switch is due' for a job the condition was never about. 'Counts work, not time' is not enough; it has to count THE work the threshold was agreed about. The same sentence I wrote about stalls applies one level up, and I did not see it until the number was half spent.	node=42504
